  "warnings": [
    "The five-hole screw-clearance callout is fully transcribed in D004 and represented by F005-F008. Its drill diameter is unspecified. The reporting schema rejects unknown/zero hole diameters, so these five holes cannot honestly be entered in hole_callouts without inventing a diameter. All five positions are explicitly preserved in F005 and F007. Resolve drill diameter, counterbore diameter, and counterbore depth before building.",
    "Do not use .375 as the clearance-hole diameter: it is the nominal screw size. The five small holes comprise three upright and two orthogonal flange holes.",
    "The smaller holes are interpreted as through clearance holes from geometry and application; only the central bore and large four-hole callout explicitly print THRU.",
    "Inches and third-angle projection are explicit. No ASME/ANSI/ISO/DIN standard is named; drawing_standard left blank.",
    "Drawing number reads 4071-C. No separate revision is populated; suffix C was retained in the number. Additional title-block model number is 180.",
    "Material reads 1020, conventionally carbon steel. Heat-treatment field is blank.",
    "Broad-face coordinates use its lower-left corner: x along 5.75, y along 4.00. Flange-hole positions use upper-left end-view lower-left coordinates: horizontal along 1.88, y along 4.00. These are different face-local coordinate systems.",
    "Hole coordinates are calculated from printed dimension chains, not scaled from the image. Derived dimensions have no additional independent tolerance; retain controlling tolerances.",
    "View names are not printed. Front upper-left, Right upper-right and Bottom lower-left provide a consistent third-angle interpretation; CAD orientation may differ without changing geometry.",
    "Datum A is the inner flange face. Perpendicularity .001 applies to the inner upright face. Frame is printed in legacy order perpendicularity | A | .001.",
    "Surface-texture values 125 and 250 are conventionally microinches; the roughness parameter is not printed.",
    "Hidden-line depiction is selective. Missing hidden outlines must not be used to infer blind holes or omit visible bores."
  ],
